# Supabase service-role credentials for author-side maintenance scripts.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE-TIME SETUP:
#   1. Copy this file to `.env.supabase`  (cp .env.supabase.example .env.supabase)
#   2. Fill in both values from the Supabase dashboard
#      (Project Settings → API — the "service_role" key, NOT the publishable one)
#   3. From then on just run `pnpm backfill:census` — it auto-loads .env.supabase
#      (via node --env-file-if-exists). No more pasting creds.
#
# `.env.supabase` is gitignored and never committed. This `.example` twin is
# safe to commit because it holds no secrets.
#
# ⚠ THE SERVICE-ROLE KEY BYPASSES RLS. It belongs on the author's machine and
# in Supabase's own function env — never in a client bundle, never in a browser.
# Used by: scripts/backfill-census.js (S7 census backfill / repair) and
#          scripts/batch-import.mjs (the catalogue batch importer).
#
# ⚠ TWO CREDENTIALS LIVE HERE AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE (learned
# 2026-08-21). SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is an HTTP key from the dashboard
# under Settings -> API Keys. EITHER generation works:
#     sb_secret_…    a new secret key (preferred; Supabase's current shape)
#     eyJ…           the legacy service_role JWT
# NOT a publishable/anon key — those are bound by RLS, and these scripts write
# rows they do not own. (The two generations are sent differently: a new secret
# key is not a JWT, so it must go on the `apikey` header only. batch-import.mjs
# detects the shape and sets the headers accordingly.) It speaks HTTP/PostgREST,
# which is what the two scripts above use. SUPABASE_DB_URL is a POSTGRES
# CONNECTION STRING (postgresql://…) used by `pnpm verify:auth` through psql.
# Pasting one where the other belongs fails in a way that reads like a missing
# credential rather than a wrong one, so batch-import.mjs now says which is
# which when it finds only the DB URL.

SUPABASE_URL=https://dtqutpdplefmufrrakxs.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
